content.text:
I started the setup you asked for. Two Haiku helpers are working in the background, and I'll check what they bring back before I write anything:

1. **README researcher:** it reads well-known READMEs from the awesome-readme list, a few research-code projects (such as Mesa) and two university guides on writing READMEs for research code.
2. **Journal auditor:** it goes through every session log and lists, with line numbers:
   - every quiz Claude gave you, and whether you answered it;
   - every time you explained an idea in your own words;
   - every bug, and whether you asked why it happened or just said "fix it";
   - every time you asked for an explanation;
   - every time you overruled a suggestion.

I already read your 296 prompts, your commits and your README myself. When the helpers finish, I'll compare their findings with my own reading and send you the full feedback. It will cover what you're doing well, what to change, rewrites of your real prompts and commit messages, whether past entries can be edited (short answer: the journal logs no, and the commit history shouldn't be either), and a README plan.

Early sources for the README part: [awesome-readme](https://awesome.ecosyste.ms/projects/github.com%2Fmatiassingers%2Fawesome-readme), [Cornell: READMEs for research code](https://data.research.cornell.edu/data-management/sharing/writing-readmes-for-research-code-software/), [RSE-UP: README files](https://se-up.github.io/RSE-UP/chapters/readme_files.html), [NYU: setting up your project](https://guides.nyu.edu/software-reproducibility/setting-up-your-project).

